<Layouts.app flash={@flash}>
  <section data-part="home">
    <header data-part="hero">
      <div data-part="hero-content">
        <noora-badge appearance="light-fill" color="primary" size="large" dot>
          Elixir framework · Hex package · MIT licensed
        </noora-badge>

        <h1>Agents you can<br /><span>actually let run.</span></h1>
        <p>
          Condukt is an Elixir framework for building agents that do real work
          on your infrastructure. Every session is a supervised process whose
          tools run in a sandbox, on a network you decide, with secrets it
          never gets to print.
        </p>

        <div data-part="hero-actions">
          <noora-button variant="primary" size="large" href={~p"/docs/framework"}>
            Build an agent
          </noora-button>
          <noora-button variant="secondary" size="large" href="#install">
            Install
          </noora-button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div data-part="hero-visual" aria-hidden="true">
        <span data-part="hero-mark">
          <span></span>
          <span></span>
          <span></span>
        </span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div id="terminal">
      {live_render(@conn, ConduktSiteWeb.TerminalLive)}
    </div>

    <section id="products" data-part="products">
      <div data-part="products-copy">
        <p data-part="eyebrow">Why a framework and not a loop</p>
        <h2>The agent loop is the easy part.</h2>
      </div>

      <div data-part="products-grid">
        <article data-part="product">
          <noora-icon name="lock" label="Isolation"></noora-icon>
          <h3>Contain the work</h3>
          <p>
            Tools run through a sandbox, not your filesystem. Take the
            in-memory one, a microVM, or a dedicated Kubernetes pod per session,
            behind one interface, so where the work runs is a deployment
            decision rather than a rewrite.
          </p>
          <a href={~p"/docs/framework/elixir/sandbox"}>Read about sandboxes</a>
        </article>

        <article data-part="product">
          <noora-icon name="server" label="Network policy"></noora-icon>
          <h3>Decide what it can reach</h3>
          <p>
            Per-session egress rules, audited and enforced outside the agent.
            Allow and deny by host, or hand the decision to another agent when
            a rule cannot say. Secrets resolve separately and are redacted from
            transcripts.
          </p>
          <a href={~p"/docs/framework/elixir/network-policy"}>Read about network policy</a>
        </article>

        <article data-part="product">
          <noora-icon name="subtask" label="OTP"></noora-icon>
          <h3>Let the platform do its job</h3>
          <p>
            Sessions are supervised processes, so streaming, backpressure,
            cancellation, and restarts come from OTP rather than from code you
            wrote. Sub-agents are children with typed inputs and outputs.
          </p>
          <a href={~p"/docs/framework/elixir/sessions-and-persistence"}>Read about sessions</a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="install" data-part="install">
      <div data-part="install-copy">
        <p data-part="eyebrow">Install</p>
        <h2>Add it to your application.</h2>
        <p>
          Condukt is a Hex package. Add it to <code>mix.exs</code>, define an
          agent module, and run it inside your own supervision tree.
        </p>
      </div>
      <condukt-install-command language="elixir" command={~s({:condukt, "~> 1.10"})}>
      </condukt-install-command>

      <div data-part="install-copy">
        <p>
          There is also a terminal coding agent built on the library, which is
          the fullest worked example of a host: it owns provider sign-in,
          workspace tools, cancellation, and presentation, and leaves
          conversation state to the session.
        </p>
      </div>
      <condukt-install-command command="mise use -g github:tuist/condukt">
      </condukt-install-command>
    </section>
  </section>
</Layouts.app>
